      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Founded in 1901 as the Academy of Idaho, the campus grew into Idaho State University, the state's second university in 1963.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6,
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://pocatello.gov/463/History",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Pocatello hosts the quirky Museum of Clean, a museum dedicated to the history of cleaning products and techniques.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pocatello%2C_Idaho",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Fort Hall Replica and Pocatello Junction Town Site recreates a 19th‑century frontier town where settlers once paused before heading west.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://visitpocatello.com/pocatello-visitors-center/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Ross Park houses the Pocatello Zoo, an aquatic complex, and a skate park, making it a hub for family fun.",
